<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- BADAN is local-first: no third-party SDKs, no analytics providers, no accounts.
	     Ingestion, scores and export generation are all on-device and open no connection.

	     CORRECTED (ADR-0011 / D-37). This block previously said "zero network calls" and
	     "nothing is ever transmitted off-device". That stopped being true when the AI coach
	     shipped (ADR-0010 / D-22), which required this correction before ship and never got
	     it. BADAN now reaches the network from exactly two of its own surfaces, both opt-in
	     and both default-off: the AI coach, and methodology telemetry.

	     NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes stays EMPTY for now, and that is a statement about what
	     ships today, not a loophole: the AI coach sends a query you typed and stores nothing,
	     and methodology telemetry (ADR-0011 / D-37) has its payload boundary and its schema
	     but no transport and no consent surface yet — nothing transmits. The moment it does,
	     this array must declare NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUsageData, purpose Analytics,
	     linked false, tracking false. A footprint carries no health value, no date and no
	     identifier, but it IS collected data sent off device, so it gets declared rather than
	     argued away. Under-declaring is an App Review risk; over-declaring is not.

	     Reading/writing the app's own on-device SQLite databases and UserDefaults remains
	     on-device processing, not "collection," which Apple's spec defines as transmission
	     off the device (or to a third party on-device).

	     A2 (ADR-0009) reviewed and deliberately changed nothing here. Background delivery and
	     BGTaskScheduler let BADAN do the SAME on-device work at a different time; they move no
	     data off the device, add no third party, and introduce no required-reason API
	     (BGTaskScheduler and HKObserverQuery are not on Apple's required-reason list). The
	     widened HealthKit read set likewise adds no collected-data type, because none of it is
	     transmitted. Padding this file with declarations that "look responsible" would make it
	     less accurate, not more — its value is that every entry below is load-bearing.

	     The standing rule: a new entry belongs here only when the app genuinely calls a
	     required-reason API or genuinely transmits something. Re-check on each release. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!-- Database.swift diskUsage() calls FileManager.attributesOfItem(atPath:) on the app's
		     own DB files (badan.db / badan_sensitive.db and -wal/-shm siblings) under
		     Application Support/BADAN to report on-device storage usage (PERF-122). File
		     timestamps of files inside the app's own container. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- FeatureFlags.swift and AppEnvironment.swift use UserDefaults.standard to store the
		     app's own preferences (onboarding-complete flag, device id, debug feature flags) —
		     never shared with or read from outside the app. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
